1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,094
<font color="#000000">Deep networks memorize training data,</font>

2
00:00:03,174 --> 00:00:06,852
<font color="#000000">and memorization ties to generalization and privacy.</font>

3
00:00:06,932 --> 00:00:10,610
<font color="#000000">Feldman's memorization score is rigorous but costly,</font>

4
00:00:10,690 --> 00:00:15,308
<font color="#000000">while input loss curvature is a cheap proxy nobody
could justify.</font>

5
00:00:15,388 --> 00:00:20,728
<font color="#000000">This Purdue ICML 2024 paper builds the missing
theory linking memorization,</font>

6
00:00:20,808 --> 00:00:24,920
<font color="#000000">curvature, and differential privacy on CIFAR and ImageNet.</font>

7
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:29,912
<font color="#000000">Deep networks memorize training data, which matters
for generalization,</font>

8
00:00:29,992 --> 00:00:32,291
<font color="#000000">noisy learning, and privacy leakage.</font>

9
00:00:32,371 --> 00:00:37,643
<font color="#000000">Feldman's memorization score quantifies this
rigorously but is far too expensive.</font>

10
00:00:37,723 --> 00:00:41,476
<font color="#000000">Input loss curvature, the trace of the input-loss Hessian,</font>

11
00:00:41,556 --> 00:00:44,978
<font color="#000000">tracks memorization roughly a thousand times cheaper,</font>

12
00:00:45,058 --> 00:00:47,622
<font color="#000000">yet the connection was purely empirical,</font>

13
00:00:47,702 --> 00:00:51,190
<font color="#000000">with no theory linking either to differential privacy.</font>

14
00:00:51,570 --> 00:00:57,648
<font color="#000000">Proving curvature bounds memorization would license
the cheap proxy over the expensive score.</font>

15
00:00:57,728 --> 00:01:00,231
<font color="#000000">Because memorization is a privacy risk,</font>

16
00:01:00,311 --> 00:01:05,462
<font color="#000000">linking differential privacy to curvature explains
why privacy reduces leakage.</font>

17
00:01:05,542 --> 00:01:11,025
<font color="#000000">Unlike influence functions, which assume Hessian
convexity that fails for deep nets,</font>

18
00:01:11,105 --> 00:01:13,740
<font color="#000000">this framework needs no such assumptions.</font>

19
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:18,886
<font color="#000000">The paper proves three links in a triangle
connecting memorization,</font>

20
00:01:18,966 --> 00:01:22,286
<font color="#000000">input loss curvature, and differential privacy.</font>

21
00:01:22,366 --> 00:01:25,396
<font color="#000000">First, curvature upper-bounds memorization.</font>

22
00:01:25,476 --> 00:01:29,808
<font color="#000000">Second, the privacy parameter upper-bounds average
curvature.</font>

23
00:01:29,888 --> 00:01:32,991
<font color="#000000">Third, privacy bounds memorization directly.</font>

24
00:01:33,071 --> 00:01:37,837
<font color="#000000">None assume Hessian convexity, so they hold for
real deep networks,</font>

25
00:01:37,917 --> 00:01:40,730
<font color="#000000">and all three are validated empirically.</font>

26
00:01:41,110 --> 00:01:46,280
<font color="#000000">The theory starts from a second-order
Nesterov-Polyak expansion of the loss,</font>

27
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:48,352
<font color="#000000">introducing the input Hessian.</font>

28
00:01:48,432 --> 00:01:51,874
<font color="#000000">A zero-mean perturbation cancels first-order terms,</font>

29
00:01:51,954 --> 00:01:58,575
<font color="#000000">and taking expectations bounds memorization by
expected curvature plus a data-independent offset.</font>

30
00:01:58,655 --> 00:02:01,614
<font color="#000000">With the definition of differential privacy,</font>

31
00:02:01,694 --> 00:02:04,446
<font color="#000000">a smaller epsilon forces lower curvature.</font>

32
00:02:04,526 --> 00:02:09,350
<font color="#000000">Curvature itself is estimated cheaply via
Hutchinson's trace estimator.</font>

33
00:02:09,730 --> 00:02:13,493
<font color="#000000">Validation uses CIFAR10, CIFAR100, and ImageNet.</font>

34
00:02:13,573 --> 00:02:19,579
<font color="#000000">Memorization relies on Feldman and Zhang's
precomputed scores and ensembles,</font>

35
00:02:19,659 --> 00:02:24,063
<font color="#000000">a thousand models on CIFAR100 and a hundred on ImageNet,</font>

36
00:02:24,143 --> 00:02:26,865
<font color="#000000">using Small Inception and ResNet50.</font>

37
00:02:26,945 --> 00:02:32,470
<font color="#000000">Privacy experiments train ResNet18 with DP-SGD
across several budgets.</font>

38
00:02:32,850 --> 00:02:34,344
<font color="#000000">Every prediction holds.</font>

39
00:02:34,424 --> 00:02:41,390
<font color="#000000">Memorization plotted against input loss curvature
shows a strong linear trend on CIFAR100 and ImageNet,</font>

40
00:02:41,470 --> 00:02:43,375
<font color="#000000">especially clean on ImageNet.</font>

41
00:02:43,455 --> 00:02:47,822
<font color="#000000">Stronger privacy drives curvature down along the
predicted curve,</font>

42
00:02:47,902 --> 00:02:50,285
<font color="#000000">and for the most-memorized examples,</font>

43
00:02:50,365 --> 00:02:56,100
<font color="#000000">memorization rises with the privacy budget while
staying under the theoretical bound.</font>

44
00:02:56,480 --> 00:03:02,108
<font color="#000000">Curvature uses Hutchinson's trace estimator with
step 1e-3 and ten Rademacher vectors,</font>

45
00:03:02,188 --> 00:03:07,816
<font color="#000000">cheap enough to run at scale.</font>

46
00:03:07,896 --> 00:03:13,524
<font color="#000000">Privacy sweeps epsilon over one, ten, twenty,
thirty, forty, and fifty at delta 1e-5,</font>

47
00:03:13,604 --> 00:03:19,232
<font color="#000000">averaging ten seeds.</font>

48
00:03:19,312 --> 00:03:24,940
<font color="#000000">The five hundred most-memorized CIFAR100 examples
collapse under strong privacy and recover as epsilon grows.</font>

49
00:03:25,320 --> 00:03:27,726
<font color="#000000">A few numbers capture the work.</font>

50
00:03:27,806 --> 00:03:34,704
<font color="#000000">Input loss curvature is about three orders of
magnitude cheaper than the Feldman score.</font>

51
00:03:34,784 --> 00:03:40,960
<font color="#000000">Ground truth averages a thousand models on CIFAR100
and a hundred on ImageNet.</font>

52
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:45,372
<font color="#000000">Privacy sweeps epsilon from one to fifty at delta 1e-5.</font>

53
00:03:45,452 --> 00:03:48,660
<font color="#000000">Three theorems tie the triangle together.</font>

54
00:03:49,040 --> 00:03:53,518
<font color="#000000">The empirical curvature-memorization link now rests
on firm theory.</font>

55
00:03:53,598 --> 00:04:00,662
<font color="#000000">Curvature provably upper-bounds memorization and is
itself bounded by the differential privacy parameter,</font>

56
00:04:00,742 --> 00:04:02,091
<font color="#000000">closing the triangle.</font>

57
00:04:02,171 --> 00:04:07,193
<font color="#000000">Needing no Hessian-convexity assumptions, it
applies to real deep networks,</font>

58
00:04:07,273 --> 00:04:12,500
<font color="#000000">so cheap curvature is a justified proxy, and
privacy provably suppresses both.</font>
